✅ 1. persist()
Belongs to: JPA

Provided by: EntityManager

Usage: To insert a new entity into the database.

It only works for new (transient) objects.

java
Copy
Edit
User user = new User();  // transient
user.setName("Ali");
entityManager.persist(user);  // now managed and inserted
Key Points:

Adds the entity to the persistence context

Entity becomes managed

Does not return anything

If the entity already exists → ❌ throws an error

✅ 2. merge()
Belongs to: JPA

Provided by: EntityManager

Usage: To insert or update an entity (commonly for detached objects).

java
Copy
Edit
User detachedUser = new User();  // detached object
detachedUser.setId(1L);          // existing ID
detachedUser.setName("Updated Name");

User managedUser = entityManager.merge(detachedUser);
Key Points:

Can be used for existing or detached objects

Returns a managed copy

Original object stays detached

If entity doesn’t exist → it creates new (INSERT)

✅ 3. save()
Belongs to: Spring Data JPA

Provided by: JpaRepository

Internally uses: persist() or merge() based on entity state

java
Copy
Edit
User user = new User();
user.setName("Awais");
userRepository.save(user);  // Spring will call persist or merge
Key Points:

Simplifies the code in Spring Boot

Handles both new and existing entities

Automatically chooses between persist() and merge()

Returns the saved (managed) entity

🔁 Summary Table
| Method      | From                  | Works on                      | Return Value   | Purpose               |
| ----------- | --------------------- | ----------------------------- | -------------- | --------------------- |
| `persist()` | JPA (`EntityManager`) | New (transient) entities      | `void`         | INSERT new record     |
| `merge()`   | JPA (`EntityManager`) | Detached or existing entities | Managed entity | UPDATE or INSERT      |
| `save()`    | Spring Data JPA       | Any (new or existing)         | Saved entity   | Handles persist/merge |


⚠️ Example Use Case
Use persist() when:

You're working directly with EntityManager

You're sure it's a new record

Use merge() when:

You're re-attaching a modified, detached entity

Use save() when:

You're using Spring Boot + Spring Data JPA

You want simple code for both insert/update